# Env transport — where this project's config lives, by machine

Project-owned. Names and paths only — **never a credential, never a
value**. This file is committed; everything in it is safe to read.

`/env-sync` uses this as a lookup so you can say "push staging to the
build box" instead of retyping an scp target. It does **not** make a
destination trusted: `/env-sync` still only sends where you explicitly
name, and this file is a convenience, not an authorization.

## Machines

| Name | Host (ssh) | Project path | Which envs live there |
|---|---|---|---|
| _(example)_ `laptop` | — (this machine) | `~/code/myproject` | local, staging |
| _(example)_ `buildbox` | `me@build.internal` | `/srv/myproject` | staging |

Delete the examples. Add a row per machine you actually work from.

## Rules

- **No values here.** If you find yourself pasting a password into this
  table, the answer is `/secrets`, not this file.
- **No production credentials on laptops** unless you mean it. An
  environment whose class is `prod` (see `.claude/environments.json`)
  should usually exist only where it is deployed.
- **`.env*` must be gitignored before any transfer.** Run
  `.claude/skills/env-sync/env-sync.sh protect` — `/secrets` only adds
  the literal `.env`, so `.env.staging` and `.env.production` are
  commonly left exposed.
- **Parity is a digest comparison**, not a merge. Two machines that
  differ need a human decision about which is right; nothing here
  resolves that for you.

